{"id":"01c73c35-f4bc-4d3b-ba50-fd75c429f5c1","object":"chat.completion","created":1784962239,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import collections\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = collections.defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = next(n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None)\\n    symbol_ids = collections.defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        symbol_ids[n['symbol']].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        candidate_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(sid in candidate_retained for sid in symbol_ids.get(sym, [])):\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n            retained = candidate_retained\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to write Python solve(case) function that takes payload as described. The task appears to be about reducing a tree, removing removable nodes while preserving required symbols, using depth-first (deeper first), tentative removal only if all required symbols remain after removal. The output should have removed_node_ids (list of IDs in order of removal) and retained_node_ids (sorted list of IDs of nodes that remain after all removals). Also need to handle tie-breaking by node ID (lexicographic? probably string compare). \n\nWe need to parse the case payload: it has nodes list, each node has id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. And required_symbols list. We need to identify root (parent null). Also need to consider that nodes may have children; we need to know parent-child relationships.\n\nAlgorithm:\n\n1. Build adjacency: map parent id to list of child nodes. Also map node id to node dict.\n2. Identify root: node with parent None (or null). \n3. Determine required symbol nodes: those with symbol in required_symbols. We need to ensure after removal, those symbols still exist in the retained tree? Actually \"all required symbols remain represented\" means at least one node with that symbol remains. So we need to check that there is at least one node for each required symbol after removal.\n\n4. We need to consider nodes in order: deeper nodes first (higher depth), then shallower. Tie break by node ID (string). So we can sort nodes by (-depth, id). But careful: we want to process nodes deeper first. Since depth is integer, we can sort by depth descending, then id ascending.\n\n5. For each node in that order, we check if it is removable, not root, not required symbol (though root might be required symbol? root symbol is \"root\" and required_symbols includes \"root\" in fixtures, so root is required. But root has removable false anyway. So we skip non-removable nodes. Also we cannot remove a node that has retained children. But what does \"retained children\" mean? In the process, children may have been removed already. So we need to ensure that after removals, the node has no children that are still retained. But also we might remove a node that has children if those children are also being removed? Actually the rule: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has any child that is still present (not removed yet or will be retained), we cannot remove it. Also, if all its children are already removed, then it's okay to remove it. Also, if a child has been removed but we haven't processed parent yet, that's fine. So we need to track which nodes are removed.\n\n6. Tentative removal: before actually removing, we need to check if all required symbols would still be represented after removal. That means considering the nodes that are currently retained (including this node). We need to see if for each required symbol, there exists at least one node of that symbol that is not removed (and not being removed now). So we need to simulate removal.\n\n7. If removal is valid, we add node id to removed_node_ids list, mark it as removed. Also we need to update the retained set (all nodes minus removed nodes). But careful: the retained list should be sorted IDs of all nodes that were not removed. At the end, we need to return sorted retained_node_ids.\n\n8. Also note: \"grammar_valid\" is mentioned but not explicitly in the payload; likely we don't need to validate grammar beyond the input is given. The \"interestingness_preserved\" is also not defined; we assume the condition about required symbols is the interestingness oracle. So we just use required_symbols.\n\n9. The order of processing: \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" We'll sort nodes by depth descending, then ID ascending.\n\n10. Also \"registered_candidate_order\" - that's the order we process candidates (nodes). So our removal order should be that processing order, but only for nodes that actually get removed. So the removed_node_ids list should be in the order we removed them (which is the order we process, skipping non-removable).\n\n11. \"stable_ties\" - tie-breaking by ID.\n\n12. We must not mutate input case, so we'll work with copies or indices.\n\nImplementation steps in solve(case):\n\n- Create dict node_map = {node['id']: node for node in case['nodes']}\n- Create children_map: defaultdict(list), for each node, if parent not None, add node id to children_map[parent]\n- Identify root by finding node with parent None. But there should be exactly one.\n- required_symbols set = case['required_symbols']\n- Create set retained_ids = set(node['id'] for node in case['nodes'])\n- Create list removed_ids = []\n- Process nodes in order: sorted_nodes = sorted(case['nodes'], key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n- For each node in sorted_nodes:\n    if node['id'] is root id: skip (root cannot be removed, also removable is false anyway)\n    if not node['removable']: skip\n    # check if node has any retained children: get children from children_map, for each child id, if child id in retained_ids, then can't remove.\n    if any(child_id in retained_ids for child_id in children_map.get(node['id'], [])):\n        continue\n    # tentative removal: check required symbols\n    # simulate removal: compute set of retained after removal = retained_ids - {node['id']}\n    # For each required symbol, check if there is at least one node with that symbol in the retained set after removal.\n    # We can precompute symbol_to_ids map: from symbol to list of node ids. Then see if any id of that symbol is in retained after removal.\n    # If all required symbols have at least one node, then proceed.\n    # Also need to consider that root is required symbol? Yes root symbol \"root\" is required, but root is not removable, so fine.\n    # So compute:\n    removed_retained = retained_ids - {node['id']}\n    ok = True\n    for sym in required_symbols:\n        # find all nodes with that symbol that are in removed_retained\n        if not any(sym_id in removed_retained for sym_id in symbol_to_ids.get(sym, [])):\n            ok = False\n            break\n    if ok:\n        removed_ids.append(node['id'])\n        retained_ids = removed_retained\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted(retained_ids)\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}\n\nNeed to handle that root may have symbol \"root\" and required_symbols includes \"root\". So we treat root as required.\n\nPotential edge: required symbols may have multiple nodes, need at least one.\n\nAlso note: \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" That's done by sorting by (depth desc, id asc).\n\nNow, ensure we import nothing beyond standard library. Use collections.defaultdict.\n\nNow, produce the implementation JSON. We must output one JSON object with key \"implementation\", value being the Python source code as a string. Escape accordingly (newlines as \\n, backslashes as \\\\, quotes as \\\"). We'll write the code as a multi-line string, then json.dumps it.\n\nBut the instruction says: \"Encode the Python source as a JSON string, escaping newlines, backslashes, and quotes as required by JSON.\" So we can write the source as a string with proper escaping.\n\nI'll write the code within triple quotes, but for JSON string we need to replace newlines with \\n. We can manually construct the string with \\n. Alternatively, use json.dumps on the source string after writing it normally, but we need to include that inside the outer JSON. Since the whole response must be a JSON object, we can generate a Python dict with key \"implementation\" and value as the code string, then json.dumps the whole thing. That will handle escaping.\n\nBut we must ensure the response is exactly one JSON object, no extra text. So we can just print the JSON.\n\nLet's write a Python script that outputs the JSON. Actually we are to output the JSON in the response. So I'll directly construct the JSON string.\n\nI'll write the solve function code:\n\n```\ndef solve(case):\n    import collections\n    nodes = case['nodes']\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\n    children = collections.defaultdict(list)\n    for n in nodes:\n        p = n.get('parent')\n        if p is not None:\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\n    root_id = next(n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None)\n    # precompute symbol->ids\n    symbol_ids = collections.defaultdict(list)\n    for n in nodes:\n        symbol_ids[n['symbol']].append(n['id'])\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\n    removed = []\n    # order: deeper first, then id\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\n        nid = n['id']\n        if nid == root_id:\n            continue\n        if not n['removable']:\n            continue\n        # check if any child is still retained\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, []))\n        if has_retained_child:\n            continue\n        # tentative removal\n        candidate_retained = retained - {nid}\n        ok = True\n        for sym in required_symbols:\n            # at least one id of this symbol must be in candidate_retained\n            if not any(sid in candidate_retained for sid in symbol_ids.get(sym, [])):\n                ok = False\n                break\n        if ok:\n            removed.append(nid)\n            retained = candidate_retained\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\n    return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_sorted}\n```\n\nMake sure to not mutate input.\n\nNow, we need to produce the JSON. I'll build the full JSON string manually with proper escaping.\n\nThe code string should be exactly as above but with proper indentation. For JSON, we need to escape backslashes and double quotes. The code contains no backslashes except in default dict and import? Actually no. It has single quotes, double quotes? We used single quotes throughout. But we used n.get('parent') which is single quotes. Also we used \"symbol_ids\" etc. No double quotes inside. So we don't need to escape double quotes. But we have newlines. So replace each newline with \\n. Also we have indentation spaces, we can keep them. But ensure no trailing spaces. We'll write the code as a single line with \\n.\n\nAlternatively, use json.dumps on the dict. In the final answer, I'll construct a Python dict, then json.dumps it, and output that. Since the response is the JSON, I can do that.\n\nBut note: The response must be exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\". So I'll compute the JSON string.\n\nI'll write the answer as Python code that prints the JSON, but here I'm the assistant, I'll just output the JSON directly.\n\nI need to ensure the implementation string is properly escaped. I'll just write the JSON.\n\nLet me write the JSON with escaped newlines.\n\nI'll produce:\n\n{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import collections\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    node_map = {n['id']: n for n in nodes}\\n    children = collections.defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        p = n.get('parent')\\n        if p is not None:\\n            children[p].append(n['id'])\\n    root_id = next(n['id'] for n in nodes if n['parent'] is None)\\n    symbol_ids = collections.defaultdict(list)\\n    for n in nodes:\\n        symbol_ids[n['symbol']].append(n['id'])\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed = []\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes, key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    for n in sorted_nodes:\\n        nid = n['id']\\n        if nid == root_id:\\n            continue\\n        if not n['removable']:\\n            continue\\n        has_retained_child = any(cid in retained for cid in children.get(nid, []))\\n        if has_retained_child:\\n            continue\\n        candidate_retained = retained - {nid}\\n        ok = True\\n        for sym in required_symbols:\\n            if not any(sid in candidate_retained for sid in symbol_ids.get(sym, [])):\\n                ok = False\\n                break\\n        if ok:\\n            removed.append(nid)\\n            retained = candidate_retained\\n    retained_sorted = sorted(retained)\\n    return {'removed_node_ids': removed, 'retained_node_ids': retained_sorted}\"\n}\n\nBut wait, the function uses single quotes for return dict keys: {'removed_node_ids': ...}. That's fine. Also note the use of next() which will raise StopIteration if no root; but payload should have one.\n\nNow, check if we need to handle case where there are no nodes? Not needed.\n\nAlso, we didn't use 'utility' field. That's fine.\n\nNow, produce the final answer.\n\nBut we should ensure that the code is correct for the given fixtures. Let's test mentally with first fixture case 0. Nodes: root (depth0, not removable, symbol root), n1 (depth1, not removable, s1), n2 (depth1, removable, s2), n3 (depth1, not removable, s0), n4 (depth2, removable, s1), n5 (depth2, not removable, s2), n6 (depth2, removable, s0). Required symbols: root, s1.\n\nSorted nodes by depth descending: depth2: n4, n5, n6 (order by id: n4, n5, n6); depth1: n1, n2, n3 (by id: n1, n2, n3); depth0: root.\n\nProcess n4 (id n4, removable, parent n2). Children? None. Check retained children: none. Tentative removal: after removal, retained set minus n4. Required symbols: root (root is retained), s1: nodes with s1: n1 and n4. After removal, only n1 remains, so s1 still present. So remove n4. removed=[n4], retained minus n4.\n\nn5: removable false, skip.\n\nn6: removable true, parent n1. Children none. Tentative removal: retained set minus n6. Required symbols: root, s1 (n1 retained) ok. Remove n6. removed=[n4,n6].\n\nDepth1: n1: removable false, skip.\nn2: removable true, parent root. Children? n4 (already removed) but for check: children.get('n2') -> ['n4'], but n4 not in retained (since removed), so no retained child. Tentative removal: retained set minus n2. Required symbols: root, s1 (n1 is s1) ok. Remove n2. removed=[n4,n6,n2].\nn3: removable false, skip.\n\nroot: skip.\n\nResult: removed=[n4,n6,n2], retained_ids = {root,